<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">SS Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">SS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> Wilhelm der Grosse was a transatlantic liner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norddeutscher-lloyd">Norddeutscher Lloyd</a>, built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> and launched in 1897. The first liner with four funnels, she won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-riband">Blue Riband</a> for the fastest Atlantic crossing in 1897, ending British dominance of the record. Converted into an auxiliary cruiser in 1914, she was sunk off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> in August of that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ss-kaiser-wilhelm-der-grosse.jpg" alt="SS Kaiser Wilhelm der Grosse" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. [August] Loeffler (1865–1946), Tompkinsville, N.Y. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_wilhelm_der_grosse_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
